([{"id":"contains-duplicate","t":"Contains Duplicate","d":"Easy","cat":"Arrays & Hashing","p":"Hash Set","lc":217,"take":"**\"Have I seen this before?\" → use a hash set.** It turns an O(n²) pair check into O(n).","hint":"You need to answer \"have I seen this value before?\" quickly for every element. Which data structure answers membership questions in O(1)?"},{"id":"valid-anagram","t":"Valid Anagram","d":"Easy","cat":"Arrays & Hashing","p":"Frequency Count","lc":242,"take":"**Anagram / permutation / same letters → compare frequency counts** (dict, Counter or a 26-length array).","hint":"Two strings are anagrams exactly when every character has the same count in both. Count them."},{"id":"two-sum","t":"Two Sum","d":"Easy","cat":"Arrays & Hashing","p":"Hash Map (complement lookup)","lc":1,"take":"**Pair that sums to target → for each x, look up `target − x` in a hash map.** If the array is sorted, use two pointers instead (Two Sum II).","hint":"For each number `x`, the partner you need is `target - x`. Can you look up \"have I already seen `target - x`, and where?\" in O(1)?"},{"id":"group-anagrams","t":"Group Anagrams","d":"Medium","cat":"Arrays & Hashing","p":"Hash Map with computed key","lc":49,"take":"**Grouping things that are \"equivalent\" → design a canonical key and use `defaultdict(list)`.**","hint":"All anagrams share some *signature*. If you can compute the same key for all anagrams, a dict of key → list does the grouping."},{"id":"top-k-frequent-elements","t":"Top K Frequent Elements","d":"Medium","cat":"Arrays & Hashing","p":"Bucket Sort / Heap","lc":347,"take":"**Top-K → heap of size k (O(n log k)).** When the values you rank by are bounded (like frequency ≤ n), **bucket sort** gives O(n).","hint":"First count frequencies. Then you need the k largest counts. A frequency can be at most n, so you can index buckets by frequency."},{"id":"encode-and-decode-strings","t":"Encode and Decode Strings","d":"Medium","cat":"Arrays & Hashing","p":"String design (length prefix)","lc":271,"prem":true,"take":"**Length-prefixing** (like network protocols) makes any byte sequence safe to frame. Empty strings and strings containing `#` still work.","hint":"A plain delimiter fails if a string contains it. What if each string tells you **how long** it is before its content starts?"},{"id":"product-of-array-except-self","t":"Product of Array Except Self","d":"Medium","cat":"Arrays & Hashing","p":"Prefix / Suffix products","lc":238,"take":"**\"Everything except i\" → combine a prefix pass and a suffix pass.** The same idea appears in Trapping Rain Water (max-left / max-right).","hint":"answer[i] = (product of everything **left** of i) × (product of everything **right** of i). Both can be built in one pass each."},{"id":"valid-sudoku","t":"Valid Sudoku","d":"Medium","cat":"Arrays & Hashing","p":"Hash Sets (rows / cols / boxes)","lc":36,"take":"**Grid with region constraints → index regions with integer division** (`r // 3, c // 3`) and use one hash set per region.","hint":"Keep a set of seen digits for each row, each column and each box. Which box is cell (r, c) in? Try `(r // 3, c // 3)`."},{"id":"longest-consecutive-sequence","t":"Longest Consecutive Sequence","d":"Medium","cat":"Arrays & Hashing","p":"Hash Set (sequence starts)","lc":128,"take":"**Need O(n) on unsorted data where sorting would help → hash set plus \"only start from the beginning of a chain\".** The nested loop is still linear overall (amortized analysis).","hint":"Put everything in a set. A number `x` is the **start** of a run only if `x - 1` is not in the set. Count upward only from starts."},{"id":"valid-palindrome","t":"Valid Palindrome","d":"Easy","cat":"Two Pointers","p":"Two Pointers (opposite ends)","lc":125,"take":"**Symmetric check (palindromes, reversing) → two pointers from both ends.** O(1) space.","hint":"Compare the first and last *valid* characters, then move inward. Skip anything that isn't a letter or a digit."},{"id":"two-sum-ii-input-array-is-sorted","t":"Two Sum II – Input Array Is Sorted","d":"Medium","cat":"Two Pointers","p":"Two Pointers (opposite ends)","lc":167,"take":"**Sorted array + pair/sum condition → two pointers from both ends.** Why it's correct: each move eliminates the pointer that can't be part of any valid pair.","hint":"The array is sorted. If the sum of the smallest and largest is too big, which one can you safely throw away?"},{"id":"3sum","t":"3Sum","d":"Medium","cat":"Two Pointers","p":"Sort + Two Pointers","lc":15,"take":"**k-Sum → sort, fix k−2 elements with loops, and solve the last pair with two pointers.** Deduplicate by skipping equal neighbours.","hint":"Sort first. Fix the first number `a`, then you need a pair summing to `-a` in the rest: that's Two Sum II. Skip equal values to avoid duplicates."},{"id":"container-with-most-water","t":"Container With Most Water","d":"Medium","cat":"Two Pointers","p":"Two Pointers (greedy shrink)","lc":11,"take":"**Two pointers + greedy elimination:** at each step, prove one end can't be in a better answer and drop it.","hint":"Start with the widest container. The water level is limited by the shorter line, so moving the taller line inward can never help. Which pointer should move?"},{"id":"trapping-rain-water","t":"Trapping Rain Water","d":"Hard","cat":"Two Pointers","p":"Two Pointers / Prefix max","lc":42,"take":"**Water / boundary problems: amount at i = min(maxLeft, maxRight) − h[i].** Prefix/suffix max arrays give O(n) time; two pointers make it O(1) space. Monotonic stack is a third valid approach.","hint":"Water above bar i = `min(tallest bar to the left, tallest bar to the right) − height[i]` (if positive). How do you get both maxima cheaply?"},{"id":"best-time-to-buy-and-sell-stock","t":"Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock","d":"Easy","cat":"Sliding Window","p":"Sliding Window / running min","lc":121,"take":"**\"Best pair i < j\" → keep a running best of the left side while scanning the right side.** Turns O(n²) into O(n).","hint":"For each day treated as the selling day, the best buying day is the cheapest day *before* it. Track that minimum as you go."},{"id":"longest-substring-without-repeating-characters","t":"Longest Substring Without Repeating Characters","d":"Medium","cat":"Sliding Window","p":"Variable Sliding Window","lc":3,"take":"**Longest/shortest substring with a constraint → variable sliding window:** expand right, shrink left while invalid, record the answer.","hint":"Keep a window with no repeats. Extend the right edge, and when a repeat appears, shrink from the left until the window is valid again."},{"id":"longest-repeating-character-replacement","t":"Longest Repeating Character Replacement","d":"Medium","cat":"Sliding Window","p":"Variable Sliding Window + counts","lc":424,"take":"**Window validity often looks like `window_size − (something you keep) ≤ budget`.**","hint":"A window can be made uniform if `window_length − count_of_most_frequent_char ≤ k`. Those are the chars you need to replace."},{"id":"permutation-in-string","t":"Permutation in String","d":"Medium","cat":"Sliding Window","p":"Fixed Sliding Window + counts","lc":567,"take":"**\"Does some substring have property P of fixed length k\" → fixed sliding window with incremental updates.**","hint":"A permutation of `s1` has the same length and letter counts. Slide a window of size `len(s1)` over `s2` and compare counts."},{"id":"minimum-window-substring","t":"Minimum Window Substring","d":"Hard","cat":"Sliding Window","p":"Variable Sliding Window (shrink while valid)","lc":76,"take":"**Minimum window → expand until valid, then shrink while valid.** (Maximum window → expand while valid, shrink while invalid.)","hint":"Expand right until the window covers `t`. Then shrink from the left as long as it **still** covers `t`, recording the smallest window. Track how many required characters are satisfied with a counter, not by comparing whole maps."},{"id":"sliding-window-maximum","t":"Sliding Window Maximum","d":"Hard","cat":"Sliding Window","p":"Monotonic Deque","lc":239,"take":"**Max/min of every window → monotonic deque.** Store indices (not values) so you can tell when they expire.","hint":"If a newer element is bigger than an older one, the older one can **never** be a window max again. Throw it away. What structure keeps candidates in decreasing order?"},{"id":"valid-parentheses","t":"Valid Parentheses","d":"Easy","cat":"Stack","p":"Stack (matching pairs)","lc":20,"take":"**Nested / matching structure → stack.** It works for brackets, HTML tags, undo operations and expression parsing.","hint":"The most recently opened bracket must be the first one closed: Last In, First Out."},{"id":"min-stack","t":"Min Stack","d":"Medium","cat":"Stack","p":"Stack (auxiliary min stack)","lc":155,"take":"**Need an aggregate (min/max) of a stack in O(1) → store the aggregate alongside each element.**","hint":"When you push, what is the minimum of the stack *at that moment*? Store it alongside the value so it's restored automatically when you pop."},{"id":"evaluate-reverse-polish-notation","t":"Evaluate Reverse Polish Notation","d":"Medium","cat":"Stack","p":"Stack (expression evaluation)","lc":150,"take":"**Postfix evaluation = stack of operands.** Watch operand order and Python's floor-division semantics.","hint":"Numbers wait on a stack. When an operator comes, it applies to the two most recent numbers."},{"id":"generate-parentheses","t":"Generate Parentheses","d":"Medium","cat":"Stack","p":"Backtracking (with constraints)","lc":22,"take":"**Generate all valid configurations → backtracking with pruning rules that keep the partial answer valid.**","hint":"Build the string one char at a time. You can add `(` while `open < n`. You can add `)` only while `close < open`."},{"id":"daily-temperatures","t":"Daily Temperatures","d":"Medium","cat":"Stack","p":"Monotonic Stack (next greater element)","lc":739,"take":"**Next greater / next smaller element → monotonic stack.** Decreasing stack for \"next greater\", increasing stack for \"next smaller\".","hint":"Keep the days that are still waiting for a warmer day on a stack. When a warm day arrives, it resolves every colder day on top."},{"id":"car-fleet","t":"Car Fleet","d":"Medium","cat":"Stack","p":"Sort + Stack","lc":853,"take":"**Process in a sorted order and compare each item with the last \"group\" → stack.** Converting to arrival *times* makes the comparison simple.","hint":"Sort cars by position, closest to the target first. Compute each car's arrival time if it were alone. A car behind that would arrive *sooner* (or at the same time) gets blocked and joins the fleet ahead."},{"id":"largest-rectangle-in-histogram","t":"Largest Rectangle in Histogram","d":"Hard","cat":"Stack","p":"Monotonic Stack (increasing)","lc":84,"take":"**\"How far can each element extend before hitting something smaller\" → monotonic increasing stack.** The same technique solves Maximal Rectangle.","hint":"For each bar, the biggest rectangle using its full height stretches left and right until a **shorter** bar. An increasing stack tells you exactly when a bar can't extend further right."},{"id":"binary-search","t":"Binary Search","d":"Easy","cat":"Binary Search","p":"Binary Search (classic)","lc":704,"take":"**Sorted + search → binary search. Get the loop invariant right:** `[lo, hi]` inclusive ↔ `while lo <= hi` ↔ `mid ± 1`.","hint":"Look at the middle. If it's too small, the target can only be on the right half."},{"id":"search-a-2d-matrix","t":"Search a 2D Matrix","d":"Medium","cat":"Binary Search","p":"Binary Search (flattened index)","lc":74,"take":"**A sorted 2D structure is often just a 1D sorted array in disguise.** Map indices with `divmod`.","hint":"Read row by row, the matrix is one long sorted array of length m·n. Index k maps to `(k // n, k % n)`."},{"id":"koko-eating-bananas","t":"Koko Eating Bananas","d":"Medium","cat":"Binary Search","p":"Binary Search on the Answer","lc":875,"take":"**\"Minimum X such that condition holds\" + monotonic condition → binary search on the answer space.** Write a `feasible(x)` helper.","hint":"If speed k works, every speed > k also works. The yes/no answer is **monotonic** in k, so binary search over k in `[1, max(piles)]`."},{"id":"find-minimum-in-rotated-sorted-array","t":"Find Minimum in Rotated Sorted Array","d":"Medium","cat":"Binary Search","p":"Binary Search (rotated)","lc":153,"take":"**Rotated sorted array → one half is always sorted.** Compare mid with an end to tell which half has the break.","hint":"Compare `nums[mid]` with `nums[hi]`. If `nums[mid] > nums[hi]`, the drop (the minimum) must be to the right of mid."},{"id":"search-in-rotated-sorted-array","t":"Search in Rotated Sorted Array","d":"Medium","cat":"Binary Search","p":"Binary Search (rotated)","lc":33,"take":"**In a rotated array, identify the sorted half first, then ask whether the target is in it.**","hint":"At any mid, at least one half (`[lo, mid]` or `[mid, hi]`) is sorted normally. Check whether the target lies inside that sorted half."},{"id":"time-based-key-value-store","t":"Time Based Key-Value Store","d":"Medium","cat":"Binary Search","p":"Binary Search (on timestamps)","lc":981,"take":"**\"Latest value at or before time t\" → keep sorted (time, value) lists and binary search (last ≤ t).**","hint":"Per key, the (timestamp, value) list is already sorted, because timestamps only increase. \"Largest ≤ t\" is a binary search."},{"id":"median-of-two-sorted-arrays","t":"Median of Two Sorted Arrays","d":"Hard","cat":"Binary Search","p":"Binary Search (partition)","lc":4,"take":"**Binary search doesn't have to be over values. Here it searches over *partition positions*.** Treat it as \"find the split where left ≤ right\".","hint":"The median splits the combined array into a left half and a right half. Choose how many elements of the **smaller** array go to the left half (binary search!), and the rest is forced."},{"id":"reverse-linked-list","t":"Reverse Linked List","d":"Easy","cat":"Linked List","p":"In-place Reversal","lc":206,"take":"**Pointer reversal = save next, flip, advance.** It's a building block for Reorder List, Reverse k-Group and palindrome lists.","hint":"Walk the list and flip each `next` pointer to point backwards. You need to remember three nodes: previous, current, and the next one before you lose it."},{"id":"merge-two-sorted-lists","t":"Merge Two Sorted Lists","d":"Easy","cat":"Linked List","p":"Dummy Node + Two Pointers","lc":21,"take":"**Dummy node trick:** whenever you build or modify the head of a list, start from a dummy.","hint":"Repeatedly take the smaller head. A **dummy** starting node avoids special-casing the first node."},{"id":"reorder-list","t":"Reorder List","d":"Medium","cat":"Linked List","p":"Fast/Slow + Reverse + Merge","lc":143,"take":"**Many hard linked-list problems = combine small primitives:** find the middle, reverse, merge.","hint":"It's three small problems: find the middle, reverse the second half, then interleave the two halves."},{"id":"remove-nth-node-from-end-of-list","t":"Remove Nth Node From End of List","d":"Medium","cat":"Linked List","p":"Two Pointers (gap of n)","lc":19,"take":"**\"k-th from the end\" → two pointers with a fixed gap of k.**","hint":"If a `right` pointer is n nodes ahead of `left`, then when `right` hits the end, `left` is just before the node to delete."},{"id":"copy-list-with-random-pointer","t":"Copy List with Random Pointer","d":"Medium","cat":"Linked List","p":"Hash Map (old → new)","lc":138,"take":"**Cloning a graph-like structure → hash map from original node to its clone.** The same idea solves Clone Graph.","hint":"The tricky part is `random`: it might point to a node you haven't copied yet. First create **all** copies (map old → new), then wire the pointers."},{"id":"add-two-numbers","t":"Add Two Numbers","d":"Medium","cat":"Linked List","p":"Linked List + carry","lc":2,"take":"**Digit-by-digit arithmetic → loop while any input or a carry remains.**","hint":"Do grade-school addition from the ones digit, which is conveniently the head. Keep a carry."},{"id":"linked-list-cycle","t":"Linked List Cycle","d":"Easy","cat":"Linked List","p":"Fast & Slow Pointers (Floyd)","lc":141,"take":"**Cycle detection in O(1) space → fast & slow pointers.** It also finds the middle of a list and the cycle start (Find the Duplicate Number).","hint":"Two runners on a circular track, one twice as fast, will eventually meet."},{"id":"find-the-duplicate-number","t":"Find the Duplicate Number","d":"Medium","cat":"Linked List","p":"Floyd's cycle (array as linked list)","lc":287,"take":"**Array with values in [1..n] → treat values as pointers (index → value).** Then use linked-list tricks.","hint":"Treat index `i` as a node pointing to `nums[i]`. Because two indices point to the duplicate value, the \"list\" has a cycle, and the entrance to the cycle is the duplicate."},{"id":"lru-cache","t":"LRU Cache","d":"Medium","cat":"Linked List","p":"Hash Map + Doubly Linked List","lc":146,"take":"**O(1) lookup + O(1) ordering → hash map + doubly linked list** (= `OrderedDict`). It's a classic design-interview question.","hint":"You need O(1) lookup (hash map) and O(1) \"move to most-recent\" / \"remove least-recent\" (doubly linked list). Combine them: the map stores pointers to list nodes."},{"id":"merge-k-sorted-lists","t":"Merge k Sorted Lists","d":"Hard","cat":"Linked List","p":"Heap (k-way merge)","lc":23,"take":"**Merging k sorted sequences → min-heap of the k current heads (k-way merge).** O(N log k).","hint":"At every step, the next node of the answer is the smallest among the k current heads. What gives you the minimum of k items quickly?"},{"id":"reverse-nodes-in-k-group","t":"Reverse Nodes in k-Group","d":"Hard","cat":"Linked List","p":"In-place Reversal (groups)","lc":25,"take":"**Group-wise reversal = the basic reversal + careful reconnection.** Draw the 4 pointers: group_prev, first, kth, group_next.","hint":"Repeat: find the k-th node from the current group's predecessor. If it exists, reverse that group and reconnect it to its predecessor and successor."},{"id":"invert-binary-tree","t":"Invert Binary Tree","d":"Easy","cat":"Trees","p":"Tree DFS (recursion)","lc":226,"take":"**Most tree problems = do something at the node + recurse on children.** Always handle `None` first.","hint":"Mirroring a tree = swap the two children of every node. Do it recursively."},{"id":"maximum-depth-of-binary-tree","t":"Maximum Depth of Binary Tree","d":"Easy","cat":"Trees","p":"Tree DFS / BFS","lc":104,"take":"**A tree property that combines children's answers → post-order DFS returning a value.**","hint":"depth(node) = 1 + max(depth(left), depth(right))."},{"id":"diameter-of-binary-tree","t":"Diameter of Binary Tree","d":"Easy","cat":"Trees","p":"Tree DFS (height + global answer)","lc":543,"take":"**The DFS returns one thing (height) but updates another (global best).** This pattern repeats in Max Path Sum and Longest Univalue Path.","hint":"The longest path through a node = height(left) + height(right). Compute heights bottom-up and track the best sum."},{"id":"balanced-binary-tree","t":"Balanced Binary Tree","d":"Easy","cat":"Trees","p":"Tree DFS (height with early exit)","lc":110,"take":"**Combine \"compute a value\" and \"validate\" in one DFS using a sentinel.** This avoids O(n²) height recomputation.","hint":"Compute heights bottom-up. Return −1 as a signal meaning \"already unbalanced\" to stop early."},{"id":"same-tree","t":"Same Tree","d":"Easy","cat":"Trees","p":"Tree DFS (parallel)","lc":100,"take":"**Comparing two trees → recurse on both in lockstep.** Base cases: both None, one None, values differ.","hint":"Walk both trees at the same time."},{"id":"subtree-of-another-tree","t":"Subtree of Another Tree","d":"Easy","cat":"Trees","p":"Tree DFS + Same Tree","lc":572,"take":"**Reuse a smaller tree function (Same Tree) inside a traversal.**","hint":"At each node of `root`, ask \"is the tree starting here the same as subRoot?\" using the Same Tree function."},{"id":"lowest-common-ancestor-of-a-binary-search-tree","t":"Lowest Common Ancestor of a BST","d":"Medium","cat":"Trees","p":"BST property","lc":235,"take":"**BST → use ordering to pick one direction (like binary search).** For a general binary tree LCA, use DFS that returns the found nodes.","hint":"In a BST, if both values are smaller than the node, both are in the left subtree. Where do they *split*?"},{"id":"binary-tree-level-order-traversal","t":"Binary Tree Level Order Traversal","d":"Medium","cat":"Trees","p":"Tree BFS","lc":102,"take":"**Level-by-level anything (right view, zigzag, averages, min depth) → BFS with a `for _ in range(len(q))` loop.**","hint":"Use a queue. At the start of each round, the queue holds exactly one level. Process `len(queue)` nodes."},{"id":"binary-tree-right-side-view","t":"Binary Tree Right Side View","d":"Medium","cat":"Trees","p":"Tree BFS (last of level)","lc":199,"take":"**\"View from a side\" → one node per level → BFS (last/first per level) or DFS with a depth check.**","hint":"You see exactly one node per level: the **last** one in left-to-right order."},{"id":"count-good-nodes-in-binary-tree","t":"Count Good Nodes in Binary Tree","d":"Medium","cat":"Trees","p":"Tree DFS (pass state down)","lc":1448,"take":"**Path-dependent conditions → top-down DFS passing state (max, sum, path) as parameters.**","hint":"Carry the maximum value seen on the path so far down the recursion."},{"id":"validate-binary-search-tree","t":"Validate Binary Search Tree","d":"Medium","cat":"Trees","p":"Tree DFS (valid range)","lc":98,"take":"**BST validity is a global constraint → pass (low, high) bounds down, or check that the in-order sequence is sorted.**","hint":"Checking only `left.val < node.val < right.val` is not enough. Every node must lie in a **range** inherited from all its ancestors."},{"id":"kth-smallest-element-in-a-bst","t":"Kth Smallest Element in a BST","d":"Medium","cat":"Trees","p":"In-order traversal","lc":230,"take":"**BST + \"sorted order / k-th\" → in-order traversal.** The iterative version lets you stop early.","hint":"In-order traversal of a BST visits values in sorted order."},{"id":"construct-binary-tree-from-preorder-and-inorder-traversal","t":"Construct Binary Tree from Preorder and Inorder Traversal","d":"Medium","cat":"Trees","p":"Divide & Conquer (recursion)","lc":105,"take":"**Preorder gives roots, inorder gives the left/right split.** Divide and conquer with index ranges plus a hash map.","hint":"preorder[0] is the root. Find it in inorder: everything to its left is the left subtree, and everything to its right is the right subtree."},{"id":"binary-tree-maximum-path-sum","t":"Binary Tree Maximum Path Sum","d":"Hard","cat":"Trees","p":"Tree DFS (gain + global answer)","lc":124,"take":"**Return \"best single branch\" to the parent, but update the global answer with \"both branches\".** This is the core trick for path problems on trees.","hint":"Like Diameter: at each node, the best path that **bends** here = node + best gain from the left + best gain from the right. But to the parent you can only offer **one** branch. Drop negative gains (use 0 instead)."},{"id":"serialize-and-deserialize-binary-tree","t":"Serialize and Deserialize Binary Tree","d":"Hard","cat":"Trees","p":"Tree DFS (preorder with nulls)","lc":297,"take":"**Any DFS order + null markers = a unique, reversible tree encoding.** Decode with the same recursive order.","hint":"Preorder traversal **with explicit null markers** uniquely determines the tree. To decode, read tokens in the same order recursively."},{"id":"implement-trie-prefix-tree","t":"Implement Trie (Prefix Tree)","d":"Medium","cat":"Tries","p":"Trie","lc":208,"take":"**Prefix queries, autocomplete, word dictionaries → Trie.** Operations cost O(word length), independent of how many words are stored.","hint":"A tree where each edge is a character. The path from the root spells a prefix. Mark nodes where a word ends."},{"id":"design-add-and-search-words-data-structure","t":"Design Add and Search Words Data Structure","d":"Medium","cat":"Tries","p":"Trie + DFS (wildcard)","lc":211,"take":"**Wildcards on a trie → DFS branching only at the wildcard positions.**","hint":"Normal letters follow one child. For `.`, you must try **every** child, which means DFS/backtracking over the trie."},{"id":"word-search-ii","t":"Word Search II","d":"Hard","cat":"Tries","p":"Trie + Grid Backtracking","lc":212,"take":"**Many words to search on the same board → put the words in a trie and do one combined DFS.**","hint":"Running Word Search I once per word repeats a lot of work. Put all words in a trie and run a single DFS from each cell that follows trie edges, so you explore all words at once."},{"id":"kth-largest-element-in-a-stream","t":"Kth Largest Element in a Stream","d":"Easy","cat":"Heap / Priority Queue","p":"Min-Heap of size k","lc":703,"take":"**k-th largest → min-heap of size k. k-th smallest → max-heap of size k** (push negatives in Python).","hint":"Keep only the k largest values seen. The smallest of those k is the answer. Which structure gives the smallest element in O(1)?"},{"id":"last-stone-weight","t":"Last Stone Weight","d":"Easy","cat":"Heap / Priority Queue","p":"Max-Heap (simulation)","lc":1046,"take":"**Repeatedly take the largest → max-heap (negate values in Python).**","hint":"You repeatedly need the largest two. Max-heap. In Python, store negatives in heapq."},{"id":"k-closest-points-to-origin","t":"K Closest Points to Origin","d":"Medium","cat":"Heap / Priority Queue","p":"Heap (top k smallest)","lc":973,"take":"**k smallest → max-heap of size k; k largest → min-heap of size k.**","hint":"Compare squared distances (no sqrt needed). Keep the k smallest with a **max-heap of size k**."},{"id":"kth-largest-element-in-an-array","t":"Kth Largest Element in an Array","d":"Medium","cat":"Heap / Priority Queue","p":"Heap / Quickselect","lc":215,"take":"**k-th order statistic: heap O(n log k) for streams; quickselect O(n) average for a fixed array.**","hint":"Min-heap of size k (O(n log k)), or **quickselect**: partition like quicksort, but recurse into only one side (O(n) on average)."},{"id":"task-scheduler","t":"Task Scheduler","d":"Medium","cat":"Heap / Priority Queue","p":"Greedy + Max-Heap (or math)","lc":621,"take":"**Scheduling with cooldowns → greedy: always pick the most frequent available item (max-heap) + a cooldown queue.**","hint":"Always run the task with the most remaining copies that is available (greedy). Track the cooling tasks in a queue with their ready time."},{"id":"design-twitter","t":"Design Twitter","d":"Medium","cat":"Heap / Priority Queue","p":"Heap (k-way merge of feeds)","lc":355,"take":"**\"Top N newest across many sorted lists\" → k-way merge with a heap.** You don't need to merge everything.","hint":"Each user's tweets are already in time order. Merging the newest tweets from several sorted lists is a k-way merge with a heap."},{"id":"find-median-from-data-stream","t":"Find Median from Data Stream","d":"Hard","cat":"Heap / Priority Queue","p":"Two Heaps","lc":295,"take":"**Running median / balancing two halves → two heaps (max-heap low, min-heap high).**","hint":"Split the numbers into a lower half and an upper half. You need the max of the lower half and the min of the upper half quickly."},{"id":"subsets","t":"Subsets","d":"Medium","cat":"Backtracking","p":"Backtracking (include / exclude)","lc":78,"take":"**Subsets = include/exclude decision tree (2ⁿ leaves).** The backtracking template: choose → recurse → un-choose.","hint":"Every element is either **in** or **out**. That's a binary decision tree of depth n."},{"id":"combination-sum","t":"Combination Sum","d":"Medium","cat":"Backtracking","p":"Backtracking (unbounded choices)","lc":39,"take":"**Combinations (order doesn't matter) → pass a start index forward.** Reuse allowed → recurse with `j`; no reuse → `j + 1`.","hint":"To avoid duplicates like [2,3,2] vs [2,2,3], only allow choosing candidates at index ≥ the last chosen index."},{"id":"permutations","t":"Permutations","d":"Medium","cat":"Backtracking","p":"Backtracking (used set)","lc":46,"take":"**Permutations (order matters) → loop over all elements each level, skipping used ones.** n! leaves.","hint":"Each position can hold any number not used yet. Track which ones are used."},{"id":"subsets-ii","t":"Subsets II","d":"Medium","cat":"Backtracking","p":"Backtracking (skip duplicates)","lc":90,"take":"**Duplicates in input → sort + skip equal neighbours at the same recursion level.** The same trick is used in Combination Sum II, 3Sum and Permutations II.","hint":"Sort so duplicates are adjacent. At the same recursion level, don't start a branch with a value you already started with."},{"id":"combination-sum-ii","t":"Combination Sum II","d":"Medium","cat":"Backtracking","p":"Backtracking (skip duplicates)","lc":40,"take":"**Combination Sum variants differ in only two places: the recursion index (j vs j+1) and duplicate skipping.**","hint":"Combine Combination Sum (start index, early break) with Subsets II (skip duplicates at the same level). Since each element is used once, recurse with `j + 1`."},{"id":"word-search","t":"Word Search","d":"Medium","cat":"Backtracking","p":"Grid DFS Backtracking","lc":79,"take":"**Path search on a grid without reusing cells → DFS + mark/unmark (backtracking).**","hint":"From each cell that matches word[0], DFS to neighbours that match the next letter. Mark cells as visited during the path, and unmark them on the way back."},{"id":"palindrome-partitioning","t":"Palindrome Partitioning","d":"Medium","cat":"Backtracking","p":"Backtracking (partition)","lc":131,"take":"**Partition problems → backtracking over \"where does the next piece end\".**","hint":"Choose where the first piece ends: `s[i:j+1]`. If it's a palindrome, recurse on the rest from j+1."},{"id":"letter-combinations-of-a-phone-number","t":"Letter Combinations of a Phone Number","d":"Medium","cat":"Backtracking","p":"Backtracking (Cartesian product)","lc":17,"take":"**All combinations of one choice per position → backtracking (a Cartesian product).**","hint":"One level of recursion per digit, branching over that digit's letters."},{"id":"n-queens","t":"N-Queens","d":"Hard","cat":"Backtracking","p":"Backtracking (constraint sets)","lc":51,"take":"**Constraint placement problems → backtracking + O(1) conflict checks with sets.** The diagonal indices r−c and r+c come up again and again.","hint":"Place exactly one queen per row. For a candidate column c in row r, check three sets: used columns, used `r − c` diagonals, and used `r + c` anti-diagonals."},{"id":"number-of-islands","t":"Number of Islands","d":"Medium","cat":"Graphs","p":"Grid DFS/BFS (flood fill)","lc":200,"take":"**Count connected regions → loop over cells + flood fill (DFS/BFS) or Union-Find.**","hint":"Scan the grid. Every time you hit unvisited land, that's a new island. Flood-fill (DFS/BFS) it so you don't count it again."},{"id":"clone-graph","t":"Clone Graph","d":"Medium","cat":"Graphs","p":"Graph DFS + Hash Map","lc":133,"take":"**Copying any graph or linked structure → hash map original → copy, registered before visiting neighbours.**","hint":"Like Copy List with Random Pointer: map original → clone, and create clones when you first see a node so cycles don't loop forever."},{"id":"max-area-of-island","t":"Max Area of Island","d":"Medium","cat":"Graphs","p":"Grid DFS (return size)","lc":695,"take":"**Flood fill that returns an aggregate (size, perimeter, sum).**","hint":"Same as Number of Islands, but the flood fill **returns** how many cells it sank."},{"id":"pacific-atlantic-water-flow","t":"Pacific Atlantic Water Flow","d":"Medium","cat":"Graphs","p":"Multi-source DFS (reverse flow)","lc":417,"take":"**\"Which cells can reach the border/target?\" → start from the targets and search in reverse (multi-source).**","hint":"Instead of asking \"where does water from this cell go?\" for every cell, go **backwards** from each ocean: climb to neighbours that are ≥ the current height."},{"id":"surrounded-regions","t":"Surrounded Regions","d":"Medium","cat":"Graphs","p":"Border DFS (reverse thinking)","lc":130,"take":"**Complement trick: find what is NOT captured (border-connected) instead of what is.**","hint":"It's hard to know if a region is surrounded, but easy to know if it's **connected to the border**. Mark those safe first."},{"id":"rotting-oranges","t":"Rotting Oranges","d":"Medium","cat":"Graphs","p":"Multi-source BFS","lc":994,"take":"**Simultaneous spreading from many starts (fire, infection, distance to nearest X) → multi-source BFS.**","hint":"All rotten oranges spread **simultaneously**. Start BFS with all of them in the queue. Each BFS level = one minute."},{"id":"walls-and-gates","t":"Walls and Gates","d":"Medium","cat":"Graphs","p":"Multi-source BFS","lc":286,"prem":true,"take":"**Distance to the nearest of many sources → multi-source BFS from all sources at once** (not BFS from each room).","hint":"Same as Rotting Oranges: start BFS from **all gates** at once. The first time BFS reaches a room is its shortest distance."},{"id":"course-schedule","t":"Course Schedule","d":"Medium","cat":"Graphs","p":"Topological Sort / Cycle Detection","lc":207,"take":"**Dependencies / ordering constraints → directed graph → topological sort. \"Is it possible?\" = \"is the graph acyclic?\"**","hint":"Model it as a directed graph b → a. It's possible exactly when the graph has **no cycle**."},{"id":"course-schedule-ii","t":"Course Schedule II","d":"Medium","cat":"Graphs","p":"Topological Sort (Kahn)","lc":210,"take":"**Topological order = BFS by in-degree (Kahn) or reversed DFS post-order.**","hint":"Kahn's algorithm already produces the order: the sequence in which nodes leave the queue."},{"id":"redundant-connection","t":"Redundant Connection","d":"Medium","cat":"Graphs","p":"Union-Find (cycle detection)","lc":684,"take":"**Dynamic connectivity / \"does this edge create a cycle\" in an undirected graph → Union-Find.**","hint":"Add edges one by one. The first edge whose endpoints are **already connected** closes a cycle, so it's redundant."},{"id":"number-of-connected-components-in-an-undirected-graph","t":"Number of Connected Components in an Undirected Graph","d":"Medium","cat":"Graphs","p":"Union-Find / DFS","lc":323,"prem":true,"take":"**Count groups → Union-Find (components = n − successful unions) or count DFS starts.**","hint":"Start with n components. Every successful union (merging two different groups) reduces the count by one."},{"id":"graph-valid-tree","t":"Graph Valid Tree","d":"Medium","cat":"Graphs","p":"Union-Find / DFS","lc":261,"prem":true,"take":"**Tree ⟺ connected + acyclic ⟺ n − 1 edges + acyclic.**","hint":"A tree on n nodes has exactly **n − 1 edges** and is **connected**, or equivalently, has no cycle."},{"id":"word-ladder","t":"Word Ladder","d":"Hard","cat":"Graphs","p":"BFS (shortest path, implicit graph)","lc":127,"take":"**Shortest number of steps in an unweighted (often implicit) graph → BFS.** Pre-bucket neighbours to avoid O(N²) comparisons.","hint":"Words are nodes, and one-letter differences are edges. \"Shortest\" in an unweighted graph means BFS. Find neighbours quickly using **patterns** like `h*t`."},{"id":"reconstruct-itinerary","t":"Reconstruct Itinerary","d":"Hard","cat":"Advanced Graphs","p":"Eulerian Path (Hierholzer)","lc":332,"take":"**Use every edge exactly once → Eulerian path → Hierholzer: DFS consuming edges + post-order + reverse.**","hint":"Using every edge exactly once is an **Eulerian path**. Hierholzer's algorithm: DFS, consuming edges, and add an airport to the route only **after** all its outgoing edges are used. Then reverse."},{"id":"min-cost-to-connect-all-points","t":"Min Cost to Connect All Points","d":"Medium","cat":"Advanced Graphs","p":"Minimum Spanning Tree (Prim)","lc":1584,"take":"**Connect everything with minimum total cost → MST (Prim with a heap, or Kruskal with sorting + Union-Find).**","hint":"Connecting everything at minimum total edge cost = **Minimum Spanning Tree**. Prim: grow a tree from one point, always adding the cheapest edge to a new point."},{"id":"network-delay-time","t":"Network Delay Time","d":"Medium","cat":"Advanced Graphs","p":"Dijkstra (shortest paths)","lc":743,"take":"**Single-source shortest path with non-negative weights → Dijkstra (min-heap).** Unweighted → BFS. Negative weights → Bellman-Ford.","hint":"Shortest time from k to each node with non-negative weights → Dijkstra. The answer is the max of those shortest times."},{"id":"swim-in-rising-water","t":"Swim in Rising Water","d":"Hard","cat":"Advanced Graphs","p":"Dijkstra (minimax path)","lc":778,"take":"**Minimize the maximum along a path (bottleneck path) → Dijkstra with max instead of +, or binary search on the answer + BFS.**","hint":"The cost of a path is the **maximum** elevation on it. Minimize that maximum: Dijkstra where a path's cost is `max(cost so far, next cell)` instead of a sum."},{"id":"alien-dictionary","t":"Alien Dictionary","d":"Hard","cat":"Advanced Graphs","p":"Topological Sort (build graph from order)","lc":269,"prem":true,"take":"**Order derived from pairwise comparisons → build a graph from adjacent pairs + topological sort.**","hint":"Compare adjacent words. The **first** differing character gives an edge `a → b` (a comes before b). Then topologically sort the letters. Watch out for a prefix case like [\"abc\", \"ab\"], which is invalid."},{"id":"cheapest-flights-within-k-stops","t":"Cheapest Flights Within K Stops","d":"Medium","cat":"Advanced Graphs","p":"Bellman-Ford (limited edges)","lc":787,"take":"**Shortest path with a limit on the number of edges → Bellman-Ford for exactly (limit) rounds, using a copy per round.**","hint":"Plain Dijkstra ignores the stop limit. Bellman-Ford relaxes all edges once per round, and after round i the prices use at most i edges. Run exactly k+1 rounds."},{"id":"climbing-stairs","t":"Climbing Stairs","d":"Easy","cat":"1-D DP","p":"1-D DP (Fibonacci)","lc":70,"take":"**DP recipe: define the state (ways to reach i) → recurrence (from i−1 and i−2) → base cases → order of computation → optimize space.**","hint":"To reach step n, your last move was from step n−1 or from step n−2. So ways(n) = ways(n−1) + ways(n−2)."},{"id":"min-cost-climbing-stairs","t":"Min Cost Climbing Stairs","d":"Easy","cat":"1-D DP","p":"1-D DP","lc":746,"take":"**\"Minimum cost to reach the end with small jumps\" → 1-D DP over positions.**","hint":"dp[i] = the minimum cost to stand on step i = min(dp[i−1] + cost[i−1], dp[i−2] + cost[i−2])."},{"id":"house-robber","t":"House Robber","d":"Medium","cat":"1-D DP","p":"1-D DP (take / skip)","lc":198,"take":"**\"Pick elements with no two adjacent\" → take/skip DP: dp[i] = max(dp[i−1], dp[i−2] + val).**","hint":"At house i: rob it (+ the best up to i−2) or skip it (the best up to i−1)."},{"id":"house-robber-ii","t":"House Robber II","d":"Medium","cat":"1-D DP","p":"1-D DP (circular → two linear runs)","lc":213,"take":"**Circular constraint → split into linear cases that exclude one end each.**","hint":"You can't take both the first and the last house. So the answer is max(rob houses 0..n−2, rob houses 1..n−1)."},{"id":"longest-palindromic-substring","t":"Longest Palindromic Substring","d":"Medium","cat":"1-D DP","p":"Expand Around Center","lc":5,"take":"**Palindromic substrings → expand around 2n−1 centers.** It's simpler than DP and uses O(1) space.","hint":"Every palindrome mirrors around a center. There are 2n−1 centers: each character (odd length) and each gap between characters (even length)."},{"id":"palindromic-substrings","t":"Palindromic Substrings","d":"Medium","cat":"1-D DP","p":"Expand Around Center (count)","lc":647,"take":"**Count palindromes → expand around centers and count each expansion.**","hint":"Same expansion as the previous problem, but count every successful expansion step."},{"id":"decode-ways","t":"Decode Ways","d":"Medium","cat":"1-D DP","p":"1-D DP (1 or 2 chars)","lc":91,"take":"**Counting ways to split a sequence into valid pieces of length 1 or 2 → Fibonacci-like DP with validity checks.**","hint":"Like climbing stairs: from position i, you can take 1 digit (if it's not '0') or 2 digits (if they form 10–26)."},{"id":"coin-change","t":"Coin Change","d":"Medium","cat":"1-D DP","p":"Unbounded Knapsack (min)","lc":322,"take":"**Minimum number of items to reach a total with unlimited reuse → unbounded knapsack DP over the total.**","hint":"Greedy (always the largest coin) fails, e.g. coins [1,3,4] for 6 → 4+1+1 but 3+3 is better. Use dp[a] = min coins for amount a."},{"id":"maximum-product-subarray","t":"Maximum Product Subarray","d":"Medium","cat":"1-D DP","p":"DP (track max and min)","lc":152,"take":"**When a sign flip can turn the worst into the best → track both the max and the min DP states.**","hint":"A negative number turns the **smallest** (most negative) product into the largest. Track both the max and the min product ending at each position."},{"id":"word-break","t":"Word Break","d":"Medium","cat":"1-D DP","p":"1-D DP (prefix segmentation)","lc":139,"take":"**\"Can the string be split into valid pieces?\" → dp over prefix lengths.**","hint":"dp[i] = can `s[:i]` be segmented? It's true if some word ends at i and `dp[i − len(word)]` is true."},{"id":"longest-increasing-subsequence","t":"Longest Increasing Subsequence","d":"Medium","cat":"1-D DP","p":"DP / Patience sorting + binary search","lc":300,"take":"**LIS: O(n²) DP is the standard answer; the O(n log n) \"tails + binary search\" is the optimization to know.**","hint":"O(n²) DP: `dp[i]` = the LIS ending at i. O(n log n): keep `tails[k]` = the smallest possible tail of an increasing subsequence of length k+1."},{"id":"partition-equal-subset-sum","t":"Partition Equal Subset Sum","d":"Medium","cat":"1-D DP","p":"0/1 Knapsack (subset sum)","lc":416,"take":"**\"Pick a subset reaching exactly a target, each item once\" → 0/1 knapsack: iterate the capacity backwards.**","hint":"If the total is odd → impossible. Otherwise, is there a subset summing to total/2? That's the classic subset-sum / 0-1 knapsack."},{"id":"unique-paths","t":"Unique Paths","d":"Medium","cat":"2-D DP","p":"Grid DP","lc":62,"take":"**Counting paths on a grid with restricted moves → dp[r][c] = sum of the cells you can come from.** One row of memory is usually enough.","hint":"paths(r, c) = paths(r−1, c) + paths(r, c−1): you arrived from above or from the left."},{"id":"longest-common-subsequence","t":"Longest Common Subsequence","d":"Medium","cat":"2-D DP","p":"2-D DP on two strings","lc":1143,"take":"**Two strings → dp[i][j] over prefixes.** This one template covers LCS, Edit Distance, Distinct Subsequences and Interleaving String.","hint":"Compare the last characters. Equal → 1 + LCS of both without them. Different → drop one character from either string and take the better result."},{"id":"best-time-to-buy-and-sell-stock-with-cooldown","t":"Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock with Cooldown","d":"Medium","cat":"2-D DP","p":"State Machine DP","lc":309,"take":"**Stock problems with rules (cooldown, fee, k transactions) → state machine DP with one variable per state.**","hint":"Each day you're in one of three states: **holding** a share, **just sold** (cooldown next), or **resting** (free to buy). Write transitions between them."},{"id":"coin-change-ii","t":"Coin Change II","d":"Medium","cat":"2-D DP","p":"Unbounded Knapsack (count combinations)","lc":518,"take":"**Counting combinations → items outer, capacity inner. Counting permutations → capacity outer, items inner.**","hint":"To count combinations (not permutations), put **coins in the outer loop** and amounts in the inner loop. Then each combination is built in a fixed coin order."},{"id":"target-sum","t":"Target Sum","d":"Medium","cat":"2-D DP","p":"DP over sums (or subset-sum math)","lc":494,"take":"**Sign assignment / reachable values → DP over a dictionary of states {value: count}.**","hint":"Track a dictionary {current_sum: number_of_ways} and process the numbers one at a time."},{"id":"interleaving-string","t":"Interleaving String","d":"Medium","cat":"2-D DP","p":"2-D DP on two strings","lc":97,"take":"**Merging two sequences while keeping their order → dp[i][j] over how much of each you've used.**","hint":"dp[i][j] = can s1[:i] and s2[:j] form s3[:i+j]? The last char of s3[:i+j] came either from s1 (s1[i−1]) or from s2 (s2[j−1])."},{"id":"longest-increasing-path-in-a-matrix","t":"Longest Increasing Path in a Matrix","d":"Hard","cat":"2-D DP","p":"DFS + Memoization (DAG DP)","lc":329,"take":"**Longest path in a DAG (moves that strictly increase) → DFS + memoization.**","hint":"Strictly increasing moves can never cycle, so the grid is a DAG. `longest(r, c)` depends only on bigger neighbours. Memoize it."},{"id":"distinct-subsequences","t":"Distinct Subsequences","d":"Hard","cat":"2-D DP","p":"2-D DP on two strings (count)","lc":115,"take":"**Counting the ways one string appears inside another → dp over suffixes (or prefixes) with use/skip transitions.**","hint":"dp[i][j] = ways to form t[j:] from s[i:]. If s[i] == t[j], you can **use** s[i] (advance both) or **skip** it. Otherwise you must skip it."},{"id":"edit-distance","t":"Edit Distance","d":"Medium","cat":"2-D DP","p":"2-D DP on two strings","lc":72,"take":"**The classic string DP: edit distance.** Learn the three transitions (delete = up, insert = left, replace = diagonal).","hint":"dp[i][j] = the edit distance between word1[:i] and word2[:j]. If the last chars match, there's no cost. Otherwise take 1 + the min of the three operations."},{"id":"burst-balloons","t":"Burst Balloons","d":"Hard","cat":"2-D DP","p":"Interval DP","lc":312,"take":"**Interval DP: dp[l][r] over sub-ranges; the trick is often to pick the element that acts LAST (or first) in the range.** Related: Matrix Chain Multiplication, Minimum Cost to Cut a Stick.","hint":"Choosing which balloon to burst **first** makes the subproblems depend on each other. Instead choose which balloon in the interval is burst **last**: its neighbours are then the fixed boundaries of the interval."},{"id":"regular-expression-matching","t":"Regular Expression Matching","d":"Hard","cat":"2-D DP","p":"2-D DP / memoized recursion","lc":10,"take":"**Pattern matching with wildcards → dfs(i, j) over both strings + memoization.** The same structure solves Wildcard Matching.","hint":"dfs(i, j): does s[i:] match p[j:]? If p[j+1] is '*': either skip `x*` entirely (j+2), or, if s[i] matches x, consume one char of s and stay at j."},{"id":"maximum-subarray","t":"Maximum Subarray","d":"Medium","cat":"Greedy","p":"Kadane's Algorithm","lc":53,"take":"**Max-sum contiguous subarray → Kadane O(n).** Variants: max product (track min too), circular (total − min subarray).","hint":"If the running sum becomes negative, it can only hurt whatever comes next, so drop it and start fresh."},{"id":"jump-game","t":"Jump Game","d":"Medium","cat":"Greedy","p":"Greedy (farthest reach)","lc":55,"take":"**Reachability with \"up to k steps\" → track the farthest reachable point.**","hint":"Track the farthest index reachable so far. If you ever stand at an index beyond it, you're stuck."},{"id":"jump-game-ii","t":"Jump Game II","d":"Medium","cat":"Greedy","p":"Greedy BFS (levels by reach)","lc":45,"take":"**Min jumps / min steps with ranges → implicit BFS over windows** (greedy).","hint":"Think in BFS levels. With k jumps you can reach some window of indices. With k+1 jumps you can reach up to the farthest point reachable from anywhere in that window."},{"id":"gas-station","t":"Gas Station","d":"Medium","cat":"Greedy","p":"Greedy (running balance)","lc":134,"take":"**Circular feasibility → total check + a single pass that resets the start when the running balance goes negative.**","hint":"If total gas < total cost → impossible. Otherwise, run a balance. When it goes negative at i, **no station between the start and i** can work as a start, so the next candidate is i + 1."},{"id":"hand-of-straights","t":"Hand of Straights","d":"Medium","cat":"Greedy","p":"Greedy + Counter (smallest first)","lc":846,"take":"**Partition into consecutive runs → process the smallest first; it's forced to start a run.**","hint":"The smallest remaining card **must** start a group (nothing smaller can contain it). Greedily build groups from the smallest value."},{"id":"merge-triplets-to-form-target-triplet","t":"Merge Triplets to Form Target Triplet","d":"Medium","cat":"Greedy","p":"Greedy (filter then combine)","lc":1899,"take":"**When an operation only increases values (max/OR), first discard anything that overshoots.**","hint":"A triplet with **any** value above the target's can never be used, because max only grows. Among the rest, can every position reach the target exactly?"},{"id":"partition-labels","t":"Partition Labels","d":"Medium","cat":"Greedy","p":"Greedy (last occurrence)","lc":763,"take":"**\"Group so that each item stays in one group\" → extend the boundary to the last occurrence (an interval-merging idea).**","hint":"Record the last index of each letter. Extend the current part's end to the furthest last-occurrence of any letter inside it. When i reaches that end, close the part."},{"id":"valid-parenthesis-string","t":"Valid Parenthesis String","d":"Medium","cat":"Greedy","p":"Greedy (range of open counts)","lc":678,"take":"**When choices make the state uncertain, track the range of possible states instead of one value.**","hint":"Instead of one open-count, track the **range** [lo, hi] of possible open counts. `*` widens the range."},{"id":"insert-interval","t":"Insert Interval","d":"Medium","cat":"Intervals","p":"Intervals (three phases)","lc":57,"take":"**Sorted intervals + one new interval → before / overlap / after, in a single pass.**","hint":"Three phases: intervals entirely **before** the new one, intervals that **overlap** it (merge them all into one), and intervals entirely **after**."},{"id":"merge-intervals","t":"Merge Intervals","d":"Medium","cat":"Intervals","p":"Sort + Merge","lc":56,"take":"**Overlapping intervals → sort by start, then compare only with the last merged interval.**","hint":"Sort by start. Then an interval can only overlap the **last** merged interval."},{"id":"non-overlapping-intervals","t":"Non-overlapping Intervals","d":"Medium","cat":"Intervals","p":"Greedy (earliest end)","lc":435,"take":"**Max number of non-overlapping intervals (or min to remove) → sort by END, greedily keep.**","hint":"Keeping as many as possible is the classic **activity selection** problem: always keep the interval that **ends earliest**, which leaves the most room for the rest."},{"id":"meeting-rooms","t":"Meeting Rooms","d":"Easy","cat":"Intervals","p":"Sort + check neighbours","lc":252,"prem":true,"take":"**Any overlap among intervals → sort + compare adjacent ones.**","hint":"After sorting by start, only neighbours can conflict."},{"id":"meeting-rooms-ii","t":"Meeting Rooms II","d":"Medium","cat":"Intervals","p":"Min-Heap of end times / sweep line","lc":253,"prem":true,"take":"**Max overlap at any moment (rooms, platforms, CPUs) → min-heap of end times, or a sweep line over sorted events.**","hint":"Process meetings by start time. Keep the end times of the meetings currently running in a min-heap. If the earliest-ending room is free by now, reuse it."},{"id":"minimum-interval-to-include-each-query","t":"Minimum Interval to Include Each Query","d":"Hard","cat":"Intervals","p":"Sort + Heap (offline queries)","lc":1851,"take":"**Many queries over intervals → sort the queries (offline) + sweep + heap.**","hint":"Answer the queries in **sorted order** (offline). As q grows, add intervals that have started (l ≤ q) to a min-heap keyed by size, and pop the ones that already ended (r < q)."},{"id":"rotate-image","t":"Rotate Image","d":"Medium","cat":"Math & Geometry","p":"Matrix (transpose + reverse)","lc":48,"take":"**Matrix rotations = combinations of transpose and reversals.**","hint":"A clockwise rotation = **transpose** (swap across the main diagonal) and then **reverse each row**."},{"id":"spiral-matrix","t":"Spiral Matrix","d":"Medium","cat":"Math & Geometry","p":"Matrix (shrinking boundaries)","lc":54,"take":"**Layer-by-layer matrix traversal → four boundaries that shrink.**","hint":"Keep four boundaries: top, bottom, left, right. Walk one side, then shrink that boundary."},{"id":"set-matrix-zeroes","t":"Set Matrix Zeroes","d":"Medium","cat":"Math & Geometry","p":"Matrix (in-place markers)","lc":73,"take":"**O(1) extra space trick: reuse part of the input (the first row/column) as bookkeeping.**","hint":"Record which rows and columns must be zeroed first, then zero them. For O(1) space, store those flags in the first row and column themselves."},{"id":"happy-number","t":"Happy Number","d":"Easy","cat":"Math & Geometry","p":"Cycle Detection (set / Floyd)","lc":202,"take":"**An iterated function on a finite set eventually cycles → detect it with a set or with fast/slow pointers.**","hint":"The sequence either reaches 1 or repeats. Detect a repeat with a set, or with fast/slow pointers."},{"id":"plus-one","t":"Plus One","d":"Easy","cat":"Math & Geometry","p":"Array arithmetic (carry)","lc":66,"take":"**Digit-array arithmetic → process from the least significant digit with a carry.**","hint":"Walk from the end. A 9 becomes 0 and carries; anything else gets +1 and you're done."},{"id":"powx-n","t":"Pow(x, n)","d":"Medium","cat":"Math & Geometry","p":"Fast Exponentiation (divide & conquer)","lc":50,"take":"**Repeated operations with an associative op → binary exponentiation (also modular power and matrix power for Fibonacci).**","hint":"x^n = (x^(n/2))² for even n. This halves n each step, giving O(log n) multiplications instead of n."},{"id":"multiply-strings","t":"Multiply Strings","d":"Medium","cat":"Math & Geometry","p":"Grade-school multiplication","lc":43,"take":"**Big-number arithmetic on strings → simulate the school method with an array of digits and carries.**","hint":"Digit i of num1 times digit j of num2 contributes to positions `i + j` and `i + j + 1` of the result (an array of length m + n)."},{"id":"detect-squares","t":"Detect Squares","d":"Medium","cat":"Math & Geometry","p":"Hash Map (counting points)","lc":2013,"take":"**Geometry counting → fix one defining element (the diagonal), derive the rest, and look them up in a hash map.**","hint":"The query point and a **diagonal** point fully define a square. Iterate over the stored points as candidate diagonals and look up the other two corners by count."},{"id":"single-number","t":"Single Number","d":"Easy","cat":"Bit Manipulation","p":"XOR","lc":136,"take":"**Pairs cancel → XOR.** Related tricks: missing number, find two unique numbers (split by a set bit).","hint":"x ^ x = 0 and x ^ 0 = x, and XOR is commutative. XOR everything together."},{"id":"number-of-1-bits","t":"Number of 1 Bits","d":"Easy","cat":"Bit Manipulation","p":"Bit tricks (n & (n−1))","lc":191,"take":"**`n & (n − 1)` removes the lowest set bit. `n & -n` isolates it.**","hint":"`n & (n − 1)` clears the lowest set bit. Count how many times you can do it before n becomes 0."},{"id":"counting-bits","t":"Counting Bits","d":"Easy","cat":"Bit Manipulation","p":"DP on bits","lc":338,"take":"**DP over integers using bit relations (i >> 1, i & (i−1)).**","hint":"`i >> 1` is i with its last bit removed, and that's a smaller number you've already computed."},{"id":"reverse-bits","t":"Reverse Bits","d":"Easy","cat":"Bit Manipulation","p":"Bit shifting","lc":190,"take":"**Building a number bit by bit: `res = (res << 1) | bit`.**","hint":"Take bits from the right of n (`n & 1`) and push them into the result from the right (`res << 1`), 32 times."},{"id":"missing-number","t":"Missing Number","d":"Easy","cat":"Bit Manipulation","p":"XOR / Math","lc":268,"take":"**One missing / one extra element among a known range → XOR or sum difference.**","hint":"The expected sum of 0..n is n(n+1)/2. Or XOR every index with every value, and everything cancels except the missing one."},{"id":"sum-of-two-integers","t":"Sum of Two Integers","d":"Medium","cat":"Bit Manipulation","p":"Bit addition (XOR + carry)","lc":371,"take":"**Addition = XOR (sum) + AND-shift (carry), repeated.** Masking simulates fixed-width integers in Python.","hint":"`a ^ b` adds without carrying, and `(a & b) << 1` is the carry. Repeat until there's no carry. In Python, mask to 32 bits to handle negative numbers."},{"id":"reverse-integer","t":"Reverse Integer","d":"Medium","cat":"Bit Manipulation","p":"Math (digit by digit + overflow)","lc":7,"take":"**Digit manipulation: `x % 10` pops the last digit, `x // 10` drops it, `res * 10 + d` pushes. Check overflow before pushing.**","hint":"Pop the digits with `% 10` and push them with `res * 10 + d`. Check for overflow **before** pushing."}]).forEach((p) => NC.push(p));
